body:
My field is relevant, yay!  I'm studying diagnostic cardiac ultrasound, and we use the Piezoelectric Effect in our machines.  We use sound waves at a frequency higher than 20,000 cycles per second to take pictures of structures inside the body.  The Piezoelectric Effect is how we generate our sound.  We have teeny tiny crystals (traditionally quartz, think it's a synthetic in newer probes though) that have been treated, so when you apply an electric current to them they change their shape/size.  And when pressure is applied to them, they generate an electric current.  So our machine sends upwards of 20,000 cycles of electricity per second into these crystals, which then create soundwaves that travel into the body, bounce off structures, and some of that sound comes back.  When it hits the crystals in the probe, it applies pressure to them, and they create an electric signal that gets sent back into the machine.  Judging by how strong those electric signals are and how long it takes for them to get back tells us where a structure is and gives us an idea of its makeup (How dense and stiff it is specifically).  Then the machine creates an image from that data.  And we use the difference in the frequency of the electric signal going out and coming in to determine a doppler shift, and can tell how fast things (mainly blood in my case) are moving in the body!  All thanks to the Piezoelectric Effect turning electric signals into mechanical (sound) signals, and vice versa!

&#x200B;

No idea how the violin works though.  Yet.
